<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>4.13.5: Graph of a Function and the Domain and Range Connection</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01102</md:content-id>
    <md:title>4.13.5: Graph of a Function and the Domain and Range Connection</md:title>
    <md:uuid>0ed57142-71f9-4908-9139-079603b0b130</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title> Cool Down Activity</title>
      <para id="para-00001">The function \(W\) gives the temperature, in degrees Fahrenheit, of a pot of water on a stove \(t\) minutes after the stove is turned on. After 30 minutes, the pot is taken off the stove.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">The graph of the function is shown.</para>
      <para id="para-00003">
          <media alt="graph of temperature of water over time">
            <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_16_12_01.svg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Is 250 in the range of function \(W\)? Explain how you know.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00005">Compare your answers:</para>
          <para id="para-00006">No, 250 is not in the range. The highest temperature is 212 degrees Fahrenheit.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00007">Describe the range of the function.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">Compare your answers:</para>
          <para id="para-00009">The range is all values between 75 and 212, the lowest and the highest temperature; \(75\leq y\leq212.\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">Does \(W(t)=0\) have a solution? Explain how you know.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00011">Compare your answers:</para>
          <para id="para-00012">No, a solution to this equation would be the time when the temperature is 0 degrees Fahrenheit, which is never the case.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content></document>